\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
GUI agents emit click coordinates. We generate instead one English instruction for a
person, fine-tuning Qwen2.5-VL-3B on AndroidControl with no new annotation. A sentence is
scored by \emph{executability}. A separate grounding model, which never sees the gold
coordinate, reads the sentence, and the step counts as correct when the predicted point
falls in the cell of the element the user touched and the action class agrees. The scale is
calibrated at both ends. Human-written references reach $75.7\%$ on $4{,}463$ touch steps,
a contentless sentence reaches $12.0\%$, and a fluent sentence written for a different
screen reaches $6.1\%$. Fine-tuning lifts the model from $47.6\%$ to $59.4\%$ at two seeds,
and a second grounder, not trained on AndroidControl, keeps the sign of that gain. We then
ask whether naming the element in a descriptor before writing the same sentence helps. That
comparison was registered over two seeds per branch, and the second treatment seed was not
run. At the seed available the descriptor branch scores $57.2\%$, $2.19$ points below the
two-seed control mean. A post-hoc split by the branch's own descriptor is exploratory:
$+5.7$ points when both the name and the point are right ($n{=}1{,}871$) and $-25.2$ when
both are wrong ($n{=}738$).
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
GUI instruction generation, vision-language models, referring expression generation,
reference-free evaluation, pre-registration
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}

Given a mobile screenshot and a user goal, a GUI agent predicts an action such as
\texttt{click(872,141)}. The same screenshot and goal can also be turned into a sentence
for a person who needs to be told what to do, for example \emph{tap the pencil icon at the top right of the note}. Systems
serving that consumer are appearing in accessibility and user-support
settings~\cite{guideme}, but the generation problem has had little quantitative treatment.
A recorded touch coordinate can be checked against a predicted one, while a sentence cannot be checked against a single reference, since many wordings identify the same button and no
dataset enumerates them.

The study is about the model, so it needs a calibrated instrument first. We use
\emph{executability}. The generated sentence goes to a separate grounding
model~\cite{uground} that never sees the gold coordinate, and the step counts as correct
when that model lands in the \emph{cell} of the element the user touched, the screen region
nearest it, and the action class agrees. Section~\ref{sec:metric} gives the calibration that justifies it in compressed form: an adequacy check, a measured ceiling, a measured floor, and sensitivity over five hit rules.

We then ask two model questions.

\emph{The first is whether automatically constructed supervision works at all.}
We build the training data from AndroidControl~\cite{androidcontrol} without collecting new annotation and without labels generated by another language model, since the scored target is the per-step instruction the dataset already records, and the descriptor slots are derived by rule. We fine-tune Qwen2.5-VL-3B~\cite{qwen25vl}
with QLoRA~\cite{lora,qlora}. Supervision built this way does work. Executability rises from $47.6$ to $59.4$ points at two seeds, and the gap survives six alternative explanations, one of which discards every
sentence that reproduces its reference verbatim.

\emph{The second is whether it helps to name the element before writing the sentence.}
The referring-expression
tradition says a description is good when a listener recovers the referent from
it~\cite{mao2016,luo2017,yu2017}, and the residual errors of the fine-tuned model are of
that kind. Of its $1{,}825$ failures, $86\%$ carry the correct action together with a
wording the grounder cannot resolve. So we registered a branch whose target is a structured descriptor (role, name, coordinate, distinguishing cue) followed by the same scored sentence,
byte for byte, as the control.

The registered estimand averages two seeds per branch. One of the four runs was not
executed, so that estimand is incomplete and stays so. At the single seed available the
treatment sits $1.93$ points below its control, inside the band declared inconclusive in
advance. The mechanism behind that number is still legible. The component adds points when
the model identifies the element correctly and subtracts them when it does not, and the
subtraction is concentrated on the steps where the descriptor gets both the name and the
point wrong. A second registered stage, a minimal-pair preference objective aimed at
identification itself, was then run against a matched supervised control. It adds $+0.63$
pp, inside the inconclusive band again.

We contribute (i) an instrument for this task calibrated at both ends, with a measured
ceiling of $75.7\%$ and a measured floor, a contentless sentence scoring $12.0\%$ and a
wrong-screen one $6.1\%$ on disjoint intervals; (ii) a supervision pipeline for GUI
instruction generation that adds no annotation of its own, its gain measured at two seeds
and its sign preserved under a second grounder not trained on this corpus; and (iii) a
pre-registered ablation of descriptor-first supervision, reported as incomplete relative to
its two-seed plan, together with an exploratory single-seed diagnosis of what governs the
prefix's value and of what the follow-up objective returned.

\section{Related Work}

\emph{GUI grounding and agents.} AndroidControl~\cite{androidcontrol} provides
$15{,}283$ human-recorded episodes with per-step instructions and gold touch coordinates. AndroidInTheWild~\cite{aitw} is the source of the $14\%$ tolerance
convention. Grounding models such as SeeClick~\cite{seeclick}, OS-Atlas~\cite{osatlas},
UGround~\cite{uground} and UI-Venus~\cite{uivenus} map a text expression to a screen point, and we use two as instruments, not baselines. In Aguvis~\cite{aguvis} a model emits a low-level instruction, but only as an intermediate representation en route to an action, and its quality is never scored. In AndroidControl the human-written per-step instruction
is an \emph{input} conditioning action prediction, while here it is the generation target and
the only scored output. Supplying a structured element list is likewise established. The AndroidControl authors fine-tune with an accessibility-derived list as input, Mind2Web~\cite{mind2web} trains over noisy candidates and Widget Captioning~\cite{widgetcap} puts the view hierarchy at the input, so we claim no novelty for that layer.

\emph{Discriminative description.} Producing a description that identifies one object
among distractors is referring-expression generation~\cite{mao2016}, and later work
places a comprehension model inside the training loop to enforce
discriminativeness~\cite{luo2017,yu2017}. The closest supervised work in the GUI domain is
Widget Captioning~\cite{widgetcap}, which identifies the problem of visually identical
widgets but trains with plain cross-entropy. What we add is narrower than that tradition:
we place discriminativeness in the \emph{training target} for GUI instructions and measure
it. Outside the GUI domain, making a model emit grounding coordinates before it answers helps
when \emph{trained} rather than prompted~\cite{gcot}, the closest prior to our branch and
the expectation our result should be read against. Inside the GUI domain,
removing the inner monologue from the Aguvis training targets costs $11.4$ points on
AndroidControl-Low, our subtraction from their ablation table~\cite{aguvis}. That target is
consumed by an action head and not handed to a person, so it does not settle the question
for our setting.

\emph{Reference-free evaluation.} Verifying generated text against an external source
instead of the generator's own beliefs is the principle behind ALOHa~\cite{aloha}, which
uses an object detector alongside the references, and perturbation checklists are an
established way of validating a metric~\cite{sai2021}. Neither of the two obvious anchors
is safe here. Human judgement is itself unreliable, since untrained evaluators separate
machine from human text at chance~\cite{clark2021}, and grounder accuracy measured on one
``best'' instruction per element overstates what these models can do~\cite{groundersunderstand}.
The term \emph{executability} is used differently in open grounded planning~\cite{ogp}, as
the share of plans whose every action appears in a given action library. That is a symbolic
lookup over text, while ours is behavioural and visual.

\section{Task, Supervision, and Branches}
\label{sec:data}

From a screenshot $I$ and a goal $q$ we produce one instruction $s$ naming the element to touch and the action to perform, addressed to a human reader.

\emph{Supervision.} No public redistribution of AndroidControl carries the human-written
instruction, the screenshot and the accessibility tree together, so we join two mirrors on
\texttt{(episode\_id, step\_id)} and take trees for $99{,}131$ screens from the same
release. An off-by-one join would corrupt every downstream label while leaving the pipeline
functional, so we test the alignment. OCR text at the gold touch point appears in the reference on $48\%$ of $n=400$ sampled steps, against $20\%$ under a shifted control. The corpus holds $64{,}567$ training and $6{,}958$ test steps over $12{,}895$ and $1{,}432$
tasks, about $64\%$ of each being touch steps and forming the scoring population. The split is held out by task with zero overlap, verified at full scale. On a random sample of $120$ of those screens only
$12.6\%$ of elements carry any name, a shortfall reported elsewhere too~\cite{chen2020}, so we pass every screenshot through OCR, used as an input feature and as a name source. The descriptor label at each touch
step is built by rule from the smallest accessibility box containing the gold point:
role from the tree, name from the tree when it passes a validity check and from OCR inside the box otherwise, the gold point on a $[0,1000]$ grid, and a distinguishing cue.
At full scale $73.6\%$ of labels carry a clear name, $22.0\%$ none, and $7.6\%$ of
target elements share their name with another element on the same screen. The construction, its two filters and its label-quality audit are reported in full
elsewhere, and we use their output here.

\emph{Branches.} We build four datasets from identical inputs, differing only in the generation target: \textbf{S1}, the sentence alone; \textbf{S2}, descriptor then
sentence; \textbf{S2r}, a false descriptor taken from a different screen with
randomised coordinates, token-length matched per example; and \textbf{S2-nopoint}, S2
without the coordinate slot. An S2 target reads
\texttt{<desc>tappable text | Search here | <point>440,91</point> | just above the text
"Coffee"</desc>} followed by the same scored sentence as S1. We check nine structural invariants after every rebuild and all nine hold at full scale. The one that matters most here is that
the scored sentence is byte-identical across branches on all $64{,}567$ examples, so a
difference between branches is a difference in what precedes the sentence and nothing
else. S2r and S2-nopoint were registered as
attribution controls and, for the same reason as the second treatment seed
(Section~\ref{sec:design}), were not run. We mark them registered but not run.

\section{Descriptor-First Supervision}
\label{sec:method}

The intervention is a change of target, not of architecture, input or inference cost.
Both branches read the same prompt, made of the goal, the reference instructions of the previous three steps and the OCR strings, and both are scored on the sentence alone, with any
descriptor stripped before scoring. S2 differs only in that the target begins with four
slots: \emph{role} (button, text field, list item), \emph{name} (the displayed string),
\emph{coordinate} (the gold touch point rescaled to $[0,1000]$), and a
\emph{distinguishing cue} (the nearest OCR string outside the box with its relative
direction).

Both choices in that target carry a cost. The coordinate slot is included because it is a
clean label at zero construction cost, and the evidence at design time indicated that
intermediate steps carrying an explicit point help while free-form verbal reasoning hurts.
The descriptor is emitted rather than consumed, so the model must produce its own
identification before writing, which makes the branch an ablation of \emph{discriminative
commitment} and not of extra input. The consequence is that a wrong name and point are
inherited by the sentence that follows them. Section~\ref{sec:diag} measures how often that
happens and what it costs.

\section{The Instrument}
\label{sec:metric}

\subsection{Definition}
Let $s$ be the generated sentence with any descriptor removed, $I$ the screenshot, $g$
the gold touch coordinate, and $E$ the set of accessibility elements on the screen after
merging elements whose centres lie within $24$\,dp ($63$ px at the modal density) of one
another or of $g$. Let $G$ be a grounding model,
independent of the model under test and without access to $g$, returning $p = G(I,s)$.
The step is correct when all three hold: \emph{(i) action}, the canonicalised action
types of $s$ and of the reference agree, twelve surface verbs mapping to a single touch
class, with touch, type, scroll, long-press and back kept distinct (with one leak,
quantified in Section~\ref{sec:alt}); \emph{(ii) polarity},
$s$ and the reference do not name opposite states of the same control, checked against
nine toggle pairs; \emph{(iii) localisation}, $p$ lies within the conventional $14\%$ tolerance of~\cite{aitw}, which we implement per axis as $|p_x - g_x| \le 0.14W$, $|p_y - g_y| \le 0.14H$ and without their same-bounding-box disjunct, \emph{and} no competing element centre is closer to $p$ than $g$ itself. The tolerance region is a
per-axis rectangle, not a disc ($\pm151$ px horizontally against $\pm336$ px vertically
on a $1080\times2400$ screen), and the point that defines the cell is the recorded touch
coordinate, not the centre of the box containing it. Condition (iii) refines the tolerance
test and never accepts a step that test rejects, so the two are not alternatives.
Executability is the proportion of touch steps satisfying all three.

The rubric is a recorded human touch coordinate and not a model's opinion, so this is not
LLM-as-judge. $G$ supplies a location, the comparison against $g$ supplies the verdict, and
the same $G$ scores every branch.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.78\columnwidth]{fig_voronoi.png}
\caption{A step on which the tolerance test alone cannot carry the judgement. The user
touched the \emph{Birthday} row, and the fine-tuned model's sentence sends the grounder
inside that row's cell. The untuned model wrote ``Click on the `Gender' section\ldots'',
and the grounder followed it to the row above, $128$ px away but still inside the
$\pm336$ px vertical tolerance, which the conventional rule scores correct and the cell
rule does not.}
\label{fig:cell}
\end{figure}

Fig.~\ref{fig:cell} shows a step on which the two rules disagree, and the choice between them is not cosmetic. With the grounder's answer displaced to the centre of the nearest competing
element, the tolerance test still awards credit in $84.3\%$ of cases (injection study,
$250$ steps), a dynamic range of about $16$ points, while the cell rule awards $2.8\%$ and
still holds a ceiling of $99.7\%$ under a $3\%$ injected offset. We report the tolerance
score alongside the headline throughout, never as the headline.

\subsection{Calibration}
Four measurements support the readings in this paper.

\emph{Adequacy.} A pass condition of median localisation error at most $3\%$ of screen
width was fixed before any compute was spent, derived from screen geometry. The tolerance
margin, half the distance from the element to be touched to the nearest other element after
merging, has median $6.2\%$ of width and 10th percentile $3.6\%$. UGround-V1-2B
attains $0.7\%$ on $300$ test steps. The condition is met, but the error distribution is bimodal (the grounder either lands near the centre or elsewhere entirely), so a percentile
condition would have been the better choice, and one declared check for a broken
installation fired. On $30.7\%$ of steps the horizontal answer is exactly screen centre,
partly a property of the data and partly the grounder abandoning the axis when it cannot identify an element. Both depress scores rather than inflate them.

\emph{Ceiling.} Passing every human-written reference through the same pipeline scores
$\mathbf{75.7\%}$ $[74.1, 77.3]$ over all $4{,}463$ touch steps. Every score below is read
against $75.7$, not against $100$. An earlier $300$-step estimate of $70.0\%$ was nearly six points low and is withdrawn as a ceiling.

\emph{Floor.} Replacing every sentence with the contentless \emph{tap the button} scores
$12.0\%$ $[9.7, 14.4]$ on a random $800$-step slice whose ceiling is $74.9\%$, so the
usable range is $62.9$ points and the metric is not carried by what the grounder can guess
from the screenshot alone. A
genuine reference sentence written for a \emph{different} step, with correct register, matched length and wrong content, scores lower still, $6.1\%$ $[4.5, 7.9]$, on disjoint
intervals. A wrong sentence sends the grounder somewhere definite and wrong, while an empty
one leaves it to a weak prior. That control also holds annotator style fixed, so the
reading that the metric rewards style does not survive it.

\emph{What the metric weighs.} On the same slice, keeping the locative clause and
replacing the element name with the generic \emph{the item} costs $28.5$ points on the
$193$ steps it touches ($b{=}3$ steps the shortened sentence resolves and the reference
does not, $c{=}58$ the reverse, $p<0.001$), against $3.5$ points for deleting
the locative clause and keeping the name on a near-identical population of $198$
($b{=}1$, $c{=}8$, $p{=}0.046$). Naming the element is worth roughly eight times saying
where it is. Meaning-preserving rewrites of the references, namely verb substitution, fronting the locative clause and both at once, move executability by $+0.35$ pp
($[-0.59, +1.29]$) over the $1{,}139$ steps they alter, with $2.6\%$ of steps changing
verdict, so the instrument is insensitive to these rewrites while sensitive to content.

\emph{The case against a reference-based score.} Scored with a content-word F1 against the single
reference, $52.8\%$ of the sentences the metric accepts from the untuned model fall below
$0.5$, against $13.9\%$ for the fine-tuned one. A reference-based score therefore charges
the two branches unequally for register, a property unrelated to identification. BLEU-4 and
ROUGE-L rank the branches in the same order, but we draw nothing from that. Neither can
locate the remaining headroom, since both score the references $100$ by construction while
executability puts them at $75.7$.

\subsection{Robustness of the instrument}
The hit rule is a choice we made, so we measured how much the result depends on it.
Rescoring three branches on $698$ steps under five rules (Euclidean disc, per-axis
rectangle, the cell rule as implemented, a cell rule seeded at the box centre, and a
nearest-box rule) gives ceilings of $80.2$, $82.2$, $74.2$, $56.6$ and $82.2$, and a
fine-tuning gain of $+12.6$, $+13.0$, $+11.7$, $+9.5$ and $+13.0$ points. The absolute level
moves by up to $26$ points, the ordering never does, and the gain stays between $9.5$ and $13.0$. On the $2{,}811$ steps where two training runs emitted byte-identical sentences, the grounder returned identical coordinates and identical verdicts on all $2{,}811$.

Intervals are nonparametric cluster bootstraps with $10{,}000$ resamples, clustered by
application where one can be attributed and by task otherwise ($G = 1{,}091$, of which
$259$ are application clusters and $832$ single-task clusters covering $59\%$ of steps;
$G_{\text{eff}} = 454.3$ under Kish's formula~\cite{kish1965}). The rule was fixed in
advance because it determines the available power~\cite{cameron2008}. The measured design effect is $1.10$, and standard errors are contrast-specific, $0.785$ pp for $\mathrm{S1} - \mathrm{Base}$, giving a minimum detectable paired effect of $2.2$ points at $80\%$ power. That figure covers instrument noise alone. A contrast across two \emph{training runs} also carries the seed term of Section~\ref{sec:design}.

\section{Experimental Design and Pre-Registration}
\label{sec:design}

All branches fine-tune Qwen2.5-VL-3B-Instruct~\cite{qwen25vl} with
QLoRA~\cite{lora,qlora} under LlamaFactory~\cite{llamafactory}: 4-bit NF4, LoRA rank $8$,
$\alpha = 16$, dropout $0.05$, adapters on \texttt{q,k,v,o,gate,up,down}, vision tower and
projector frozen, context $2560$, effective batch $16$, learning rate $1\times10^{-4}$,
cosine schedule, two epochs, on a single A100 ($8{,}072$ steps, $\approx 23$ h). One configuration file serves every branch, four keys changing (dataset, seed, output directory, workers). The trainable parameter count is $14{,}966{,}784$, confirming the vision tower stayed frozen, and losses on an L4 and an A100 agree to three digits with identical \texttt{total\_flos}.

Inference is greedy, one sentence per step, with the descriptor stripped by a fixed regular expression so no branch is scored on text the others cannot produce. The untuned model reads the same prompt template, byte-identical across branches and between training and inference. Because the history carries human-written sentences rather than an action log, scoring is teacher-forced on context, which conditions absolute scores but not branch differences.

The design was committed to version control before any training run and fixes the reading
of all four outcomes in advance: a $\Delta$ whose interval excludes zero and exceeds the
seed noise floor is positive; one whose interval touches zero is a trend; an interval
covering zero with $|\Delta|$ below the detectable effect is inconclusive; a negative upper
bound is harm. The registered quantity is
$\Delta = \mathrm{mean}(\mathrm{S2}) - \mathrm{mean}(\mathrm{S1})$ over \emph{two seeds
per branch}. The operative threshold is $2.8$ pp and the inconclusive band is
$-2.8 \ldots +1.7$ pp, both edges fixed before any score existed. That threshold combines
the bootstrap standard error of the seed pair, $0.38$ pp, with a seed term of $0.46$ pp,
doubled because it rests on one degree of freedom. It is the band rule for
$\mathrm{S2} - \mathrm{S1}$ and it is the widest of the three we could have written down.
Section~\ref{sec:mindesc} reads the stage-2 pair $\mathrm{MIN} - \mathrm{CE2}$ against the
undoubled counterpart of $2.11$ pp, which fits a design carrying \emph{one} seed per branch
and lets the seed term enter as $\sqrt{2}\,\sigma$. The registered two-seed figure is
$1.67$ pp. The verdict is the same under all three. Amendments are appended with dates and never edited in place. Twenty-seven are recorded, twenty-one before
any executability score existed. The six later ones share a direction: each moved the
headroom upward and the threshold downward. The first of them moved against us as well,
cutting the control branch's share of the ceiling from $84.4\%$ to $78.0\%$.

The second seed of the treatment branch was not run, a budget decision taken on 23 August
2026. The registered two-seed test was therefore never performed, and the single-seed
comparison reported below is not a substitute for it.

